    "field": "North Atlantic climate variability",
    "problem": "It remains unclear what fraction of decadal and longer North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) variability is potentially predictable, as its physical nature differs substantially between short (<30 years) and long (>30 years) timescales, which are often conflated in standard analyses, obscuring sources of predictability.",
    "method": "This study demonstrates physical differences between North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) variability on interannual-decadal (<30 years) and multidecadal (>30 years) timescales by applying Empirical Mode Decomposition and linear regression analysis to reanalysis and model data to investigate potential predictability.",
    "task": "The task is to demonstrate the physical differences between North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) variability on interannual-decadal (<30 years) and multidecadal (>30 years) timescales, showing how the former relates to jet stream latitude and the latter to jet stream strength.",
