        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic basin",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As the leading pattern of atmospheric circulation variability over the North Atlantic, the North Atlantic Oscillation\u00b9 (NAO) has a strong influence on surface climate across the Atlantic basin and beyond (Thompson and Wallace 2001).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "On the dec\u00ad adal timescale, however, these patterns are shifted south, so that both temperature and precipitation anomalies are focused on western-central Europe.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Figure 4 also shows strong differences in Arctic climate on the two timescales, though this should be treated with caution.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "To summarise, there is evidence that NAO variability on both timescales has links to the Pacific sector. This is par\u00ad ticularly clear on the shorter timescale, where both MSLP and storm tracks show good agreement between the dif\u00ad ferent datasets.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "extreme windstorms",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Western Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "On the multidecadal timescale the positive NAO is instead associated with increased wave breaking on both sides of the jet, which is consistent with the strengthening and extension of the jet. Such large-scale conditions are known to foster the occurrence of extreme windstorms over Western Europe (Hanley and Caballero 2012; G\u00f3mara et al. 2014).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The North Atlantic is generally cool, as expected from the negative correlation with the AMO, but the pattern is noisy and the large values elsewhere are hard to interpret and may not be physically related.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "These results also provide strong evidence for the pres\u00ad ence of some forcing on the decadal NAO from more slowly varying components of the climate system than the atmosphere. Some evidence of links to Atlantic Ocean vari\u00ad ability were revealed, although other factors may also con\u00ad tribute. This evidence is particularly clear in the HiGEM GCM, where variations in the Atlantic Meridional Over\u00ad turning Circulation lead to significant SST anomalies in the subpolar gyre region which are then damped by the heat fluxes to the atmosphere.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
